---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import {FAQSection, RelatedArticles} from '../../components/article';

const title = 'Best Coworking Spaces in Lisbon 2025: A Digital Nomad\'s Guide';
const description = 'Discover Lisbon\'s top coworking spaces for digital nomads. Compare prices, amenities, locations, and vibes to find your perfect workspace in Portugal\'s capital.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2024-12-22');
const readingTime = 13;
const image = '/images/unsplash/photo-1497366216548-37526070297c-1200w.webp';
const imageAlt = 'Modern coworking space with people working on laptops';

const headings = [
  {depth: 2, slug: 'introduction', text: 'What the Scene Looks Like'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'top-coworking-spaces', text: 'Ten Spaces Worth Checking'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'comparison', text: 'How They Compare'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'day-pass-vs-monthly', text: 'Day Pass or Monthly?'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'neighborhoods', text: 'Neighborhoods'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'cafe-alternatives', text: 'Cafe Options'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'faq', text: 'Questions'}
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'What does coworking cost in Lisbon?',
    answer: 'Day passes run fifteen to thirty euros. Monthly hot desk memberships cost one hundred to two hundred. Dedicated desks run two hundred to three fifty. Private offices start around four hundred to six hundred monthly for small teams. Neighborhood and amenities affect price.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Need to book ahead?',
    answer: 'Day passes usually walk-in works but booking helps during busy times. Monthly memberships need a tour and signup. Some popular spots have waitlists for dedicated desks.'
  },
  {
    question: 'WiFi speed?',
    answer: 'Professional spaces typically have one hundred to five hundred Mbps symmetric fiber with backup. Test speed during a trial day before committing monthly.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I take calls?',
    answer: 'Most spaces have phone booths or meeting rooms. Hot desks in open areas work for quick calls but not great for long video meetings. Some have dedicated call zones.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Good for meeting people?',
    answer: 'Definitely. Many Lisbon spaces host events and workshops and social stuff. Places like Second Home and Heden have strong communities. Great way to build a network.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Just need workspace sometimes?',
    answer: 'Day passes work perfectly. Some offer punch cards at ten days discounted or part-time memberships at ten to fifteen days monthly. Cafes are free but less reliable for long stretches.'
  }
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Portugal Digital Nomad Visa Guide',
    excerpt: 'Everything you need to work legally from Lisbon.',
    href: '/guides/digital-nomad-visa-portugal',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1555881400-74d7acaacd8b-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Lisbon Portugal cityscape'
  },
  {
    title: 'Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere',
    excerpt: 'Tips for finding fast internet while traveling.',
    href: '/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1544197150-b99a580bb7a8-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'WiFi connectivity concept'
  },
  {
    title: 'Top Destinations for Digital Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Compare Lisbon with other nomad hotspots.',
    href: '/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1488646953014-85cb44e25828-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'World travel destinations'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="guides/travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Lisbon turned into a nomad hotspot and the coworking scene blew up with it. Spent four months
    there trying different spaces. From converted warehouses to fancy design spots here's what
    actually matters when picking a workspace in Portugal's capital.
  </p>

  <h2 id="introduction">What the Scene Looks Like</h2>

  <p>
    Dozens of coworking spaces all over the city. Every one has its own vibe. Want quiet and
    professional? There's that. Social community hub? Got it. Nice view? Yep.
  </p>

  <p>
    Spaces cluster in a few neighborhoods. Chiado and Baixa are central. Santos feels trendy.
    Principe Real is nice but pricier. Alcantara has waterfront stuff. Prices and vibes vary
    wildly so test a few before committing to anything monthly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="top-coworking-spaces">Ten Spaces Worth Checking</h2>

  <h3>Heden</h3>
  <p><strong>Great for meeting people.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Heden has a tight community of freelancers and remote workers. Events happen regularly.
    Rooftop terrace. Easy to make friends there. Attracts creative types and startup people.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Santos. About one seventy five monthly for hot desk. Day pass runs twenty euros.
    The rooftop alone makes it worth visiting.
  </p>

  <h3>Second Home Lisboa</h3>
  <p><strong>For people who care about aesthetics.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Part of the global Second Home network and honestly stunning inside. Plants everywhere.
    Colorful furniture. Natural light streaming in. You pay more for the premium experience though.
  </p>
  <p>
    Near Time Out Market at Mercado da Ribeira. Two fifty to three fifty monthly. Day pass
    is thirty. Beautiful design and global network access if you travel to other cities.
  </p>

  <h3>Cowork Lisboa</h3>
  <p><strong>When budget matters.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Basic no-frills coworking that's centrally located. Everything you need and nothing extra.
    If basics and location beat fancy amenities this is it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Baixa and Chiado. One hundred to one fifty monthly. Day pass just fifteen euros.
    Central and cheap and does the job.
  </p>

  <h3>Avila Spaces</h3>
  <p><strong>River views and modern setup.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sits in the Avila tower with Tagus River views. Modern professional facilities. Multiple
    floors with different options from hot desks to private suites. Established businesses
    and serious freelancers like this one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Parque das Nacoes. One fifty to two hundred monthly. Day pass twenty to twenty five.
    The views and parking are nice perks.
  </p>

  <h3>Idea Hub</h3>
  <p><strong>Startup focused.</strong></p>
  <p>
    More incubator than pure coworking. Attracts founders and early-stage companies. Good for
    connecting with the Portuguese tech scene and investors.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Saldanha. One thirty to one eighty monthly. Day pass around eighteen. Mentorship and
    investment connections if you're building something.
  </p>

  <h3>Village Underground Lisboa</h3>
  <p><strong>Creative vibes.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Built from shipping containers and old buses. Most unique space in Lisbon by far. Creative
    artistic atmosphere draws designers and artists and anyone wanting workspace inspiration.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Alcantara. About one seventy five monthly. Day pass twenty two. The architecture and
    outdoor space are unlike anything else.
  </p>

  <h3>LACS</h3>
  <p><strong>Multiple spots around town.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Several locations make LACS convenient wherever you're staying. Professional and flexible.
    Safe reliable choice when you don't want to overthink it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Various locations including Conde d'Obidos and Anjos. One forty to two hundred monthly.
    Day pass eighteen to twenty five. Flexibility is the main draw.
  </p>

  <h3>Sitio</h3>
  <p><strong>Events and social stuff.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Coworking plus an active events calendar. If workshops and talks and social gatherings
    sound good this place delivers both work and community.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Principe Real. One fifty to one ninety monthly. Day pass around twenty. Regular events
    and good neighborhood and a terrace.
  </p>

  <h3>Selina Secret Garden</h3>
  <p><strong>Coliving plus coworking.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Part of the Selina network offering both rooms and workspace. Good all-in-one option when
    you first arrive. Garden area for outdoor work is pleasant.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Intendente. Around one fifty monthly just for coworking. Day pass twenty. Coliving
    option and hostel vibe.
  </p>

  <h3>Impact Hub Lisbon</h3>
  <p><strong>Social impact focused.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Part of the global Impact Hub network. Attracts social entrepreneurs and sustainability
    types. Strong programming around impact topics.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Beato. One sixty to two hundred monthly. Day pass twenty. Mission-driven community
    and global network.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison">How They Compare</h2>

  <h3>By Price Monthly Hot Desk</h3>
  <p>
    Budget one hundred to one fifty. Cowork Lisboa.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mid-range one fifty to two hundred. Heden and LACS and Avila Spaces and Idea Hub and
    Sitio and Impact Hub.
  </p>
  <p>
    Premium two hundred plus. Second Home and Village Underground.
  </p>

  <h3>By Vibe</h3>
  <p>
    Social community. Heden and Sitio and Selina.
  </p>
  <p>
    Professional quiet. Avila Spaces and LACS.
  </p>
  <p>
    Creative. Village Underground and Second Home.
  </p>
  <p>
    Startup. Idea Hub and Impact Hub.
  </p>

  <h3>By Location</h3>
  <p>
    Central. Cowork Lisboa and Second Home and LACS.
  </p>
  <p>
    Waterfront. Avila Spaces and Village Underground.
  </p>
  <p>
    Trendy. Heden in Santos and Sitio in Principe Real.
  </p>

  <h2 id="day-pass-vs-monthly">Day Pass or Monthly?</h2>

  <p>
    Day passes make sense when you're there less than two weeks. Or testing multiple spaces.
    Or only need workspace one or two days weekly. Or mixing cafe work with occasional coworking.
  </p>

  <p>
    Monthly makes sense staying a month plus. Needing workspace most days. Wanting community
    and networking. When day passes add up to more than monthly.
  </p>

  <h3>Simple math</h3>
  <p>
    Day pass twenty. Monthly one fifty. Break-even is seven to eight days. More than eight
    days monthly and membership saves money.
  </p>

  <h2 id="neighborhoods">Neighborhoods</h2>

  <h3>Chiado and Baixa</h3>
  <p>
    Central and touristy but convenient. Good for short stays near everything. Higher rents
    but walkable to lots of spaces and restaurants and transport.
  </p>

  <h3>Santos and Cais do Sodre</h3>
  <p>
    Trendy riverside with good nightlife. Popular with nomads and young professionals. Heden
    is here. Near Second Home. Good local and international mix.
  </p>

  <h3>Principe Real</h3>
  <p>
    Upscale quiet green. Nice gardens and cafes. Slightly pricier. Good for residential feel
    while staying central.
  </p>

  <h3>Alfama and Mouraria</h3>
  <p>
    Historic hilly charming. Fewer coworking spots but authentic character. Good for cafe
    workers who don't mind steep hills.
  </p>

  <h3>Alcantara and Belem</h3>
  <p>
    Waterfront with converted industrial spaces. Village Underground and LX Factory are here.
    Bit removed from center but great for creative types.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cafe-alternatives">Cafe Options</h2>

  <p>
    Don't need coworking every day. Some reliable cafes for laptop work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Copenhagen Coffee Lab. Multiple spots. Great coffee. Work-friendly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fabrica Coffee Roasters. Specialty coffee. Outlets available. Quiet mornings.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hello Kristof. Nomad favorite. Good WiFi. Relaxed about laptops.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ler Devagar at LX Factory. Bookstore cafe. Unique atmosphere. Good for shorter sessions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wish. Healthy food. Spacious. Work-friendly vibe.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cafe etiquette matters. Buy something every couple hours. Skip peak lunch. Be aware when
    you're taking up tables during busy times.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Pick Your Spot</h2>

  <p>
    Lisbon coworking has something for everyone. Grab day passes at two or three spaces
    matching what matters to you. Community or location or price or vibe. Find your fit
    then go monthly and settle in.
  </p>

  <p>
    Best space is the one you'll actually use and enjoy. In Lisbon you've got options.
  </p>

  <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
</ArticleLayout>
